course: everyday
lesson: 407
title: Rage-quitting, ranked games and the calm jar
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Kai's team was losing a Fortnite match, and Kai felt the heat rise up his neck. He pressed «quit». For one second it felt brilliant. Then he pictured his four friends, still in the match, now four against five. They lost. The next day the squad played without him, and nobody said why, because nobody had to. Rage-quitting feels good for one second and costs much more afterwards.
explain: Ranked games give you a badge — bronze, silver, gold — that goes up when you win and down when you lose. Priya dropped a rank after five losses and decided to play until she won it back. At 11pm she had lost two more ranks, her eyes were stinging and she slept badly. Chasing a lost rank while you are upset usually loses more. Theo, meanwhile, loved his gold badge more than his school report, until his mum asked what the badge actually does, and all Theo could say was «it's... gold».
explain: Being «tilted» means you are so annoyed you start playing worse and worse. When Ollie got tilted, his team laughed, which made him angrier. Then Mia said «take five». He walked to the kitchen, drank some water, came back and laughed with them. Leo did not take five: he slammed his laptop shut, a thin crack appeared across the screen, and his dad was very much not laughing.
explain: Jayden found the cure. He keeps a «calm jar»: one marble for every match he finishes without shouting. After a month it was full, and his dad took him for ice cream. Then Zara's dad shouted at his own ranked game, and Zara said, very sweetly, «Dad. Calm jar.» He laughed and put a marble in. And Kai? He once unplugged the router mid-match so the loss «wouldn't count». His sister's online lesson cut out, the whole house lost the internet, and the game counted the loss anyway.
explain: Noah blamed his teammates for every loss, until they watched the replay together: he had been knocked out first every single time. It is easier to see other people's mistakes than your own. And Ruby was too nervous to play ranked at all, until she tried one ranked game with two friends sitting beside her. She lost, and found it was fine.
fact: Rage-quitting means leaving a match in anger before it ends.
fact: When one player quits a team game, the rest of the team plays one person short.
fact: In ranked games your rank goes up when you win and down when you lose.
fact: Playing on while upset to win a rank back usually leads to more losses.
fact: Being «tilted» means being so annoyed that you play worse and worse.
fact: A short break of about five minutes calms most anger down.
fact: Standing up and walking away is safer than hitting or slamming things in anger.
fact: Games usually still count a match as lost if a player disconnects or quits.
fact: Counting a good habit, like with a jar of marbles, makes it easier to keep.
fact: Grown-ups get angry at games too, and they can learn calm tricks from children.
fact: It is easier to see other people's mistakes than your own.
exercise: Why did Kai's friends stop inviting him after he rage-quit? | He left them four against five and they lost | They thought he had lag and kept dropping out of games | He won the match without them | His mum told them not to invite him any more
exercise: How did rage-quitting feel to Kai? | Good for one second, then guilty | Good for the whole evening | Scary from the very start | Nothing at all
exercise: What happened when Priya played on to win her rank back? | She lost two more ranks and slept badly | She got her rank back by bedtime and felt great | She reached the top rank | Her rank stayed exactly the same all night
exercise: What is a wise rule after losing two ranked games in a row? | Stop and come back tomorrow | Play faster and faster until you win one | Play all night to make up for it | Switch to a harder mode to get better
exercise: What could Theo say his gold badge does? | Nothing, except that it is gold | It gets him better marks at school | It makes him a bit of money every month | It lets him skip homework
exercise: What does «tilted» mean in games? | So annoyed that you play worse and worse | Holding the controller at a funny angle while you play | Winning lots of games in a row | Playing on a laptop that is not flat
exercise: What helped Ollie when he was tilted? | Taking five minutes away from the game | His team laughing louder and louder at him | Playing ten more matches angry until he won | Turning the volume right up
exercise: What happened when Leo slammed his laptop shut? | The screen cracked a little | The game felt sorry and gave him the win back | His rank went up because the game ended | Nothing, laptops cannot break
exercise: How does Jayden's calm jar work? | One marble for each match finished without shouting | One marble for every single match he wins, however he plays | One marble for each hour he plays | One marble for every player he beats
exercise: What did Zara do when her dad shouted at his game? | Said «Dad, calm jar», and he laughed | Shouted back at him even louder than he did | Unplugged his console | Joined in the shouting
exercise: Noah blames his teammates for every loss. What did the replay show? | He died first every time | His teammates never really played at all, just stood still | The other team was cheating | The game was broken that night
exercise: Why did unplugging the router go so badly for Kai? | The house lost internet and the loss still counted | The router caught fire and the whole house was cross | He was banned for a year | It won him the match
exercise: Ruby is too nervous to play ranked. What helped her? | Trying one ranked game with friends beside her | Playing ranked alone at midnight, when nobody is watching | Never trying ranked ever | Letting her cousin play it for her
exercise: What is a wise thing to do when you feel the heat rising in a match? | Take a breath, finish, then step away | Quit quickly before the loss can count | Throw the controller at the sofa cushion | Unplug the internet before the match ends
exercise: Which is a kind of courage in ranked games? | Finishing a match you are losing | Quitting before the loss shows up on your rank | Blaming everyone else loudly | Hiding your rank from friends
exercise: Why are tricks to dodge a loss usually a bad idea? | They often cause a bigger loss | They always work perfectly, every time | Games reward them with extra points | Nobody ever notices them, so they're pointless
test: «we're losing 4 nil, i'm leaving lol» — what happens to the squad? | They play one short and probably lose | They win, because there's less lag | The game stops for everyone | Nothing, the bot plays perfectly
test: «lost my rank, i'm playing till i get it back even if it's 2am» | Bad idea, stop now and try tomorrow | Good idea, practice makes perfect | Good idea, ranks reset at 2am so you'll get it back | Fine if you drink energy drinks
test: squad: «Ollie's TILTED, look at him lol». What does Ollie need? | A five-minute break away from the game | More laughing from the squad to cheer him up | To play ten more angry matches | To mute everyone for ever
test: Leo's fist is up over the laptop lid. Best thing right now? | Stand up and walk away from it | Slam it, he'll feel much better after | Shut it softly then punch the bed really hard | Throw the charger across the room instead
test: Kai: «if i pull the plug the loss won't count». Truth? | Games usually count it as a loss anyway | True, it's a clever trick that everyone uses | True, but only on Fridays | It counts as a win instead
test: Noah: «my team's rubbish, every loss is THEIR fault». Wise reply? | Watch the replay and check your own play too | Yeah, find a new team every single game | Shout at them till they get better | Report them all for losing so they get banned
test: Zara's dad yells «are you KIDDING me» at the telly. What's true? | Grown-ups rage too and can learn calm tricks | Only kids ever get angry at games, never adults | Dads are never allowed to play | He's right to yell, it helps
test: «how's a jar of marbles gonna help, that's for babies» — why does it work? | Counting a good habit makes it easier to keep | Marbles make you better at aiming somehow | It's a magic jar | It doesn't, it's just there as decoration for the shelf
test: Theo: «gold badge > school report». Which is true? | A rank is fun but it's only a number in a game | A badge is worth more than learning anything | Gold badges get you into university later on | School reports are for babies
test: Ruby: «ranked's too scary, i'll stay in practice forever». Kind advice? | Try one with friends beside you, it's ok to lose | Never go near ranked, ever, it's too dangerous | Play ranked alone at night | Ask someone else to play it for you
test: «it felt amazing quitting, why's everyone being off with me?» | They lost because you left them a player short | They're jealous of your rank | They didn't notice you left | It's because you won the match and they didn't
exam: Identify the principal consequence for the remaining members of a team when one participant abandons a match in anger. | The remaining members are compelled to continue with fewer players | The match is cancelled without result for all participants | The remaining members are awarded the victory | The abandoning participant is credited with a win
exam: Whereas a pupil has sustained several consecutive defeats in a ranked game, select the course of action most consistent with sound judgement. | Play ought to be discontinued and resumed upon a subsequent day | Play ought to be continued without interruption until the former rank is restored | The difficulty ought to be increased forthwith | The internet connection ought to be severed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition designated «tilted»? | A state of annoyance in which performance progressively deteriorates | A state of exceptional skill following several consecutive victories | A defect in the positioning of the display | A penalty imposed by the game operator
exam: State the outcome consistent with the established facts where a participant disconnects the household router to avert a recorded defeat. | The defeat is ordinarily recorded notwithstanding the disconnection | The defeat is erased from all records held by the game operator | The participant is awarded additional points | The opposing team is penalised instead
